<article class="env-lemma" id="00K0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00K0">Lemma <span data-tag="00K0">10.58.6</span>.</a> If $M$ is a finitely generated graded $S$-module, and if $S$ is finitely generated over $S_0$, then each $M_ n$ is a finite $S_0$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose the generators of $M$ are $m_ i$ and the generators of $S$ are $f_ i$. By taking homogeneous components we may assume that the $m_ i$ and the $f_ i$ are homogeneous and we may assume $f_ i \in S_{+}$. In this case it is clear that each $M_ n$ is generated over $S_0$ by the “monomials” $\prod f_ i^{e_ i} m_ j$ whose degree is $n$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>